# 
# https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#hasSpatialLocus
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#hasSpatialLocus> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#ObjectProperty> .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#hasSpatialLocus> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#inverseOf> <https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#isSpatialLocusOf> .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#hasSpatialLocus> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#domain> <https://cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/7.1.1/E5_Event> .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#hasSpatialLocus> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#range> <https://cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/7.1.1/Place> .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#hasSpatialLocus> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "This property relates an Event to a spatial anchor—physical or digital—used to situate the event in space when such contextualization is meaningful. It is intentionally broad and optional: many events can be spatially located in principle, but the location may be unknown, irrelevant, or not recorded. Use hasSpatialLocus for lightweight contextual placement (e.g., an interaction occurring in a messaging platform, an assignment made within an institutional system, a reaction recorded via a device), without implying that the event has a formal venue or that spatial information is a primary descriptive feature.\n\nTypical use: contextual, “if needed” spatialization of any event (including non-programmed or non-operational events)."@en .
