_:genid145 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#first> <https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Techno-Place> .
_:genid145 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#List> .
_:genid143 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#takesPlaceAt> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "This property relates an Activity (including both Process and Planned_Activity) to its broader place of occurrence—the territorial or techno-spatial context in which the activity unfolds. It is used to record the general geographic or infrastructural setting of an activity (e.g., a city, region, country, or a digital/technical environment), independently of the specific host site. In this model, takesPlaceAt complements hasVenue: while hasVenue points to the concrete hosting site (e.g., a museum building, gallery, institutional facility, or platform-as-site), takesPlaceAt captures the wider territorial or techno-place context that situates that venue and the activity within a larger spatial frame.\n\nTypical use:\n\nExhibition hasVenue Villanueva building (Museo del Prado) (site)\n\nExhibition takesPlaceAt Madrid (territorial entity)\n\nOnline activities may use takesPlaceAt to indicate the relevant techno-place (e.g., a platform/ecosystem) when the broader infrastructural context matters."@en .
