_:genid192 <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#allValuesFrom> <https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Date> .
_:genid192 <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#onProperty> <https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#hasTimeSpan> .
_:genid192 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Restriction> .
<https://cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/7.1.1/E6_Destruction> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "The \"Destruction\" class represents the process and act of causing material objects to become damaged, dismantled, or rendered unusable. This includes any activities that lead to the degradation, disintegration, or obliteration of objects, whether intentional or accidental. The Destruction class is crucial for documenting and understanding the loss or alteration of material objects within various contexts, such as art conservation, historical documentation, and cultural heritage preservation."@en .
<https://cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/7.1.1/E6_Destruction> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "destrucción"@es .
<https://cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/7.1.1/E6_Destruction> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "destruction"@en .
# 
# https://cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/7.1.1/E74_Group
<https://cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/7.1.1/E74_Group> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/7.1.1/E74_Group> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Collective_Human_Actant> .
<https://cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/7.1.1/E74_Group> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "This class comprises human actors organized into groups, such as artist groups, who act collectively in their roles or activities within the art exhibition domain, but does not necessarily have a legal or corporate structure. This class has a different scope from the CIDOC-CRM E74 Group class, which also includes institutions. In the context of the OntoExhibit ontology, institutions constitute a distinct class as they are recognized to have a legal personality different from that of groups, with a more complex and formal structure, and specific functions that are regulated and acknowledged by society. See the “Institution” class as a subclass of “Corporate Body”."@en .
<https://cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/7.1.1/E74_Group> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "group"@en .
<https://cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/7.1.1/E74_Group> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "grupo"@es .
<https://cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/7.1.1/E74_Group> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#equivalentClass> "http://xmlns.com/foaf/spec/#term_Group"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#anyURI> .
# 
# https://cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/7.1.1/Place
<https://cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/7.1.1/Place> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/7.1.1/Place> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "Place represents any spatial or locative context in which events, practices, or cultural processes are situated. It covers a broad spectrum of locations, ranging from geopolitical entities (e.g., countries, regions, cities) and built environments (e.g., buildings, rooms, exhibition halls) to techno-digital environments (e.g., online platforms, virtual spaces, networked interfaces). The class is intended to support the description of the multiple spatial conditions under which art is produced, curated, mediated, displayed, and experienced. Place is broader than CIDOC CRM’s E53 Place, which is restricted to physical space. OntoExhibit maintains compatibility with CRM by allowing physical places to align with E53 where appropriate, while also accommodating techno-digital places that fall outside CRM’s strictly physical scope."@en .
<https://cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/7.1.1/Place> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "Within OntoExhibit, Place functions as a general umbrella category that supports more specific, role-oriented subclasses. In particular, Site captures places in their capacity as exhibition venues (i.e., where an exhibition is presented to audiences), while Process_Locus captures places in their capacity as loci of cultural processes (e.g., where conception, curation, creation, foundation, dissolution, or destruction are situated). A given place may play none, one, or multiple such roles depending on the relationships asserted in the knowledge graph; therefore, these subclasses should not be read as fixed “types of place,” but as analytically meaningful contextualizations of place within exhibition ecosystems."@en .
<https://cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/7.1.1/Place> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "lugar"@es .
<https://cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/7.1.1/Place> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "place"@en .
# 
# https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Academy_Of_Fine_Arts
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Academy_Of_Fine_Arts> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Academy_Of_Fine_Arts> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Cultural_Institution> .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Academy_Of_Fine_Arts> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "academia de bellas artes"@es .
