# 
# https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Actant
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Actant> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Actant> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "This class comprises entities that engage in a role or perform specific activities, either on their own initiative or mobilized by other entities. In the context of the OntoExhibit ontology, it refers to those entities that play an active role in the art exhibition domain. This class is broader than the CIDOC-CRM E39 Actor class, which is limited to human entities. Additionally, the decision to use the term \"actant\" over the more conventional term \"actor\" stems from the Greimasian tradition, where the condition of being an actant is determined by the specific role or function that an entity performs. In contrast, the term \"actor,\" as currently associated with the concept of actor in Bruno Latour's actor-network theory, is defined by its capacity to exert agency. Acknowledging that any entity might possess agency within a specific context or time, the class labeled as “actor” would be all-encompassing, including all classes. Conversely, designating a distinct class as \"actor\" would restrict agency value to those classes classified as subclasses of actor."@en .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Actant> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "actant"@en .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Actant> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "actante"@es .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Actant> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#equivalentClass> "http://xmlns.com/foaf/spec/#term_Agent"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#anyURI> .
# 
# https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Activity
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Activity> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Activity> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E7_Activity> .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Activity> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "This class comprises specific, goal-oriented actions and operations carried out by a single agent or a collective. Activities are concrete, distinguishable events situated in a spatio-temporal context, typically involving coordination, resources, and participation. They may lead to material or conceptual outcomes, and include both transformative processes (e.g., creation, production, curation) and planned or programmed activities (e.g., concerts, lectures, openings)."@en .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Activity> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "actividad"@es .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Activity> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "activity"@en .
# 
# https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Activity_Place
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Activity_Place> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Activity_Place> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#professional_context> .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Activity_Place> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "Place of Activity is an intermediary (reified) spatio-temporal context that links a human actant (or collective) to one or more places in which they carry out professional activities during a defined time range. It is intended to capture activity geographies that may be distributed across multiple locations rather than reducible to a single, fixed base. For example, an art gallery may operate through branches, fairs, collaborations, or recurring programs in several cities beyond the city where its main office is located.\n\nIn OntoExhibit, Place of Activity supports the analysis of dispersed cultural work and the spatial dynamics of professional trajectories. The set of places connected to an actant through successive or concurrent instances of Place of Activity constitutes the actant’s broader area of activity, enabling comparative study of mobility, networked operations, and the territorial and techno-digital reach of actors within exhibition ecosystems."@en .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Activity_Place> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "activity place"@en .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Activity_Place> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "lugar de actividad"@es .
# 
# https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Affiche
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Affiche> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Affiche> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Visual_Information_Object> .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Affiche> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "affiche"@en .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Affiche> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "póster"@es .
# 
# https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Affiliation
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Affiliation> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Affiliation> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#professional_context> .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Affiliation> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "Affiliation is an intermediary (reified) spatio-temporal context that records a formal staff relationship between an individual and an organisation. It captures cases in which a person is affiliated with an institution through employment or an equivalent contractual arrangement, performing defined roles and responsibilities within the organisation during a specific time range (and, where relevant, under particular terms or statuses).\n\nIn OntoExhibit, Affiliation supports the analysis of institutional labour and professional trajectories by making staff relationships explicit, time-qualified, and queryable, without reducing them to a timeless link between a person and an organisation."@en .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Affiliation> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "affiliation"@en .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Affiliation> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "afiliación"@es .
