<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Auction_House> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Auction_House> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Company> .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Auction_House> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "auction house"@en .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Auction_House> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "casa de subastas"@es .
# 
# https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Author
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Author> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Author> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Creator> .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Author> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "The \"Author\" class represents individuals or entities responsible for the creation and conceptualization within art exhibitions. This includes not only the creators of the exhibited works, such as visual artists, sculptors, painters, photographers, and multimedia artists, but also those involved in the conceptualization of the exhibition itself, the design of the museographic elements, and other creative and productive contributions that shape the overall experience of the exhibition."@en .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Author> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "author"@en .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Author> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "autor"@es .
# 
# https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Beginning_Of_Activity
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Beginning_Of_Activity> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Beginning_Of_Activity> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Planned_Activity> .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Beginning_Of_Activity> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "The \"Beginning of Activity\" class represents the initial actions or events that mark the commencement of a larger activity or event. This includes ceremonies, inaugural speeches, opening performances, or any other activities that signify the start of an event or activity."@en .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Beginning_Of_Activity> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "beginning of activity"@en .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Beginning_Of_Activity> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "inicio de actividad"@es .
# 
# https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Biennial
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Biennial> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Biennial> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Exhibition> .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Biennial> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "The \"Biennial\" class represents a large-scale exhibition event that occurs every two years, showcasing a curated selection of artworks, often from a diverse range of artists, disciplines, and cultural backgrounds. Biennials are significant events in the art world, designed to present cutting-edge contemporary art, foster international cultural exchange, and engage with global artistic trends. These events typically span several months and include various programs such as exhibitions, performances, panel discussions, workshops, and educational activities."@en .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Biennial> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "bienal"@es .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Biennial> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "biennial"@en .
# 
# https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Biological_Event
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Biological_Event> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Biological_Event> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/7.1.1/E5_Event> .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Biological_Event> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "A biological event refers to any significant occurrence or process within a biological system that results in a change or development in the organism, population, or ecosystem."@es .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Biological_Event> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "biological event"@en .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Biological_Event> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "evento biológico"@es .
# 
# https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Biological_Object
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Biological_Object> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Biological_Object> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Non_Produced_Material_Object> .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Biological_Object> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "The \"Biological Object\" class represents entities derived from living organisms, including plants, animals, fungi, and microorganisms. These objects are naturally occurring and can be displayed in art exhibitions for their scientific, educational, aesthetic, or cultural significance. Biological Objects may include specimens, preserved samples, live organisms, or parts of organisms (such as leaves, bones, shells, or feathers) and can be integral components of artworks or installations, providing a unique intersection between art and nature."@en .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Biological_Object> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "biological object"@en .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Biological_Object> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "organismo biológico"@es .
