# 
# https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Digital_Place
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Digital_Place> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Digital_Place> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Techno-Place> .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Digital_Place> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "This class comprehends a variety of digital environments such as digital platforms, websites, virtual reality (VR) environments, augmented reality (AR) spaces, and other online interfaces where technology facilitates interaction or presentation. In OntoExhibit, “digital places” also represent the digital environments where digital cultural activities, included digital exhibitions, take place. “Digital Places” may act as “Digital Sites”."@en .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Digital_Place> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "digital place"@en .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Digital_Place> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "espacio digital"@es .
# 
# https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Digital_Platform
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Digital_Platform> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Digital_Platform> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Digital_Place> .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Digital_Platform> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "The \"Digital Platform\" class represents large-scale technological infrastructures designed to host, manage, and support extensive digital interactions and activities. These platforms provide the backbone for a wide range of digital services and applications, facilitating complex operations and high-volume user engagement. Digital Platforms include large-scale social media networks, comprehensive cloud computing services, extensive content distribution networks, and other robust digital infrastructures that go beyond specific websites. In the art exhibitions domain, an example of a digital platform could be Europeana, which features a dedicated section for the presentation of digital exhibitions."@en .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Digital_Platform> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "digital platform"@en .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Digital_Platform> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "plataforma digital"@es .
# 
# https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Digital_Site
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Digital_Site> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Digital_Site> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#equivalentClass> _:genid207 .
_:genid207 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
_:genid207 <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#intersectionOf> _:genid209 .
_:genid209 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#rest> _:genid208 .
_:genid208 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#rest> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#nil> .
_:genid208 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#first> <https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Site> .
_:genid208 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#List> .
_:genid209 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#first> <https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Digital_Place> .
_:genid209 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#List> .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Digital_Site> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Site> .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Digital_Site> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "The \"Digital Site\" class represents specific, smaller-scale digital locations that are part of larger digital environments or platforms. These sites include individual web pages, subdomains, sections of websites, or specific interactive components within a broader digital platform. “Digital Sites” serve focused purposes within their larger digital contexts, such as hosting specific content -including digital exhibitions-, and facilitating targeted interactions, or providing specialized services."@en .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Digital_Site> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "digital site"@en .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Digital_Site> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "sitio digital"@es .
# 
# https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Disciple
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Disciple> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Disciple> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Role> .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Disciple> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "disciple"@en .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Disciple> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "discípulo"@es .
# 
# https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Discourse
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Discourse> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Discourse> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Proposition> .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Discourse> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "An articulated set of propositions that constitutes a discursive framework (conceptual, narrative, or argumentative) through which a phenomenon in the exhibition ecosystem is defined, justified, or interpreted."@en .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Discourse> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "discourse"@en .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Discourse> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "discurso"@es .
# 
# https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Dispositif
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Dispositif> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Dispositif> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "In OntoExhibit, the \"Dispositif\" class represents a category that encompasses entities functioning as elements for documenting exhibitions, communicating information about them, facilitating their understanding, expanding their ideas, and more. This class includes various types of dispositifs such as documentation tools, communication materials, mediation activities, interpretative aids, and expansion apparatuses. A dispositif serves to enhance engagement, comprehension, expansion, and dissemination of knowledge related to exhibitions, making it a crucial component in the art exhibition domain’s discursive and social practices. Although in OntoExhibit this class is specifically designed to codify information about exhibitions, it can also be applied to other events and objects."@en .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Dispositif> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "dispositif"@en .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Dispositif> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "dispositivo"@es .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Dispositif> <https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#modelingNote> "In OntoExhibit, Dispositif classes are not intended to be mutually exclusive. A single dispositif instance may be typed under multiple functional subclasses (e.g., Communication_Dispositif and Mediation_Dispositif) when it simultaneously performs more than one function in the exhibition ecosystem. Likewise, the distinction between institutional and non-institutional dispositifs is used as a provenance facet—indicating origin, authorization, or production context—rather than as an essential ontological kind, even though it is represented through subclassing for practical querying and organization. Hybrid provenance is modeled by multiple typing: the same dispositif instance may be asserted both as Institutional Dispositif and Non-Institutional Dispositif when production, authorship, or authorization is mixed or co-produced. Undetermined cases may be left unclassified."@en .
# 
# https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Documentation_Dispositif
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Documentation_Dispositif> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Documentation_Dispositif> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Dispositif> .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Documentation_Dispositif> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "The \"Documentation Dispositif\" class represents propositional objects used to document or register information, data, evidences, features, etc. with the aim of recording and preserving information about something. Classes that are susceptible to be documented are events, material objects, conceptual works, and places. Various propositional objects are typically employed for these purposes, including catalogs, records, inventories, databases, and other structured documentation systems that capture, organize, and store information. Additionally, other propositional objects can also serve this purpose, such as artworks, maps, diagrams, personal photograph albums, socially-built archives, etc., although they have not been primarily conceived or designed for this end. Therefore, this class links entities with those propositional objects that document them regardless their nature, genre, or typology."@en .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Documentation_Dispositif> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "dispositivo de documentación"@es .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Documentation_Dispositif> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "documentation dispositif"@en .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Documentation_Dispositif> <https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#modelingNote> "This class is not disjoint with other dispositif types; see Dispositif modeling note."@en .
