<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Editor> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "editor"@en .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Editor> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "editor"@es .
# 
# https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Educational_Institution
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Educational_Institution> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Educational_Institution> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Institution> .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Educational_Institution> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "This class comprises institutions whose main role is the education but that play a significant role in the art exhibition domain promoting, organizing or funding exhibitions."@en .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Educational_Institution> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "educational institution"@en .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Educational_Institution> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "institución educativa"@es .
# 
# https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Emotional_Reaction
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Emotional_Reaction> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Emotional_Reaction> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/7.1.1/E5_Event> .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Emotional_Reaction> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "The \"Emotional Reaction\" class represents the emotional responses elicited by artworks, exhibitions, events, or cultural entities. This includes the range of emotions experienced by individuals, such as joy, sadness, anger, surprise, and awe, in response to artistic and cultural stimuli. Emotional Reactions are important for understanding the affective impact of cultural content on audiences and can provide valuable insights into audience engagement and reception."@en .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Emotional_Reaction> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "emotional reaction"@en .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Emotional_Reaction> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "reacción emocional"@es .
# 
# https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#End_Of_Activity
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#End_Of_Activity> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#End_Of_Activity> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Planned_Activity> .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#End_Of_Activity> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "The \"End of Activity\" class represents the concluding actions or events that mark the termination of a larger activity or event. This includes closing ceremonies, final speeches, concluding performances, or any other activities that signify the end of an event or activity."@en .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#End_Of_Activity> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "end of activity"@en .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#End_Of_Activity> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "fin de actividad"@es .
# 
# https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Exact_Date
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Exact_Date> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Exact_Date> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Date> .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Exact_Date> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "The \"Exact Date\" class represents a specific point in time, defined with precision by a particular calendar date."@en .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Exact_Date> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "exact date"@en .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Exact_Date> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "fecha exacta"@es .
# 
# https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Executive_Position
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Executive_Position> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Executive_Position> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#professional_context> .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Executive_Position> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "Executive Position is an intermediary (reified) spatio-temporal context that models the formal appointment through which an individual holds a leadership role within an organisation. It captures the fact that executive authority is institutionally defined and time-bound, enabling the association of a person, an organisation, and the relevant role or office with a specific time range (and, where applicable, with additional qualifiers such as mandate type, status, or scope of responsibility).\n\nIn OntoExhibit, Executive Position covers cases in which an individual—such as a company CEO, the president of a professional association, or a museum director—embodies organisational authority and is accountable for strategic direction, decision-making, and institutional representation (including speaking on behalf of the organisation). By modelling these relations explicitly, the class supports the analysis of governance structures, institutional trajectories, and leadership networks within exhibition ecosystems."@en .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Executive_Position> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "executive position"@en .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Executive_Position> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "puesto ejecutivo"@es .
# 
