# 
# https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Information_Object
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Information_Object> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Information_Object> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Propositional_Object> .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Information_Object> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "Modeling note: Informational Object is used to assert an informational reading of an entity (it is treated as an information-bearing vehicle). This typing is not exclusive: the same entity may also be typed under other classes when a different modeling commitment is intended (e.g., when it is modeled as a publishable/citable manifestation). In ambiguous cases such as catalogs and databases, use Informational Object for their informational function, and add the additional typing only when the modeling explicitly addresses publication framing (edition/release/versioning), circulation, and reception."@en .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Information_Object> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "The \"Informational Object\" class represents entities that primarily consist of data or information, designed to convey knowledge, facts, instructions, or messages. Informational Objects can include documents, digital files, databases, manuals, maps, and other forms of recorded information. They serve as vehicles for the storage, transmission, and presentation of knowledge across various mediums and formats. This class is not equivalent to the E73 Information Object class of the CIDOC-CRM model, as in this reference model, this class is considered a subclass of Conceptual Object, thereby ignoring their material conditions."@en .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Information_Object> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "information object"@en .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Information_Object> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "objeto de información"@es .
# 
# https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Institution
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Institution> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Institution> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://www.iflastandards.info/fr/frbr/frbroo#F11_Corporate_Body> .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Institution> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "This class comprises non-profit corporate bodies. In the context of the OntoExhibit ontology, it refers those institutions that play a significant role in the art exhibition domain in its multiple dimensions. Instances of this class are Museo del Prado, Google Art and Culture, Europeana, Library of Congress, etc."@en .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Institution> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "institución"@es .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Institution> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "institution"@en .
# 
# https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Institutional_Communication_Dispositif
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Institutional_Communication_Dispositif> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Institutional_Communication_Dispositif> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Communication_Dispositif> .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Institutional_Communication_Dispositif> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "The \"Institutional Communication Dispositif\" class represents official materials and events used by institutions to communicate and disseminate information about cultural activities and productions. These dispositifs function as authoritative sources of information, providing legitimacy and formal recognition to the messages conveyed. Examples include institutional press releases, official brochures, institutional websites, social media channels managed by the institution, newsletters, and other structured communication systems."@en .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Institutional_Communication_Dispositif> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "dispositivo de comunicación institucional"@es .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Institutional_Communication_Dispositif> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "institutional communication dispositif"@en .
# 
# https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Institutional_Documentation_Dispositif
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Institutional_Documentation_Dispositif> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Institutional_Documentation_Dispositif> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Documentation_Dispositif> .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Institutional_Documentation_Dispositif> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "The \"Institutional Documentation Dispositif\" class represents official documentation tools and mechanisms used by institutions to record, organize, and legitimize information about events, activities, and objects. These dispositifs function as authoritative sources of information, providing legitimacy and formal recognition to the documented entities. Examples include official records, registries, institutional databases, and certified archival materials. The institutional nature of these dispositifs underscores their role in establishing and maintaining authoritative narratives and histories within the institutional framework."@en .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Institutional_Documentation_Dispositif> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "dispositivo de documentación institucional"@es .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Institutional_Documentation_Dispositif> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "institutional documentation dispositif"@en .
# 
# https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Institutional_Expansion_Dispositif
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Institutional_Expansion_Dispositif> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Institutional_Expansion_Dispositif> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Expansion_Dispositif> .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Institutional_Expansion_Dispositif> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "The \"Institutional Expansion Dispositif\" class represents official materials and activities used by institutions to expand the interpretations, readings, discourses, ideas, and understanding of cultural events and objects. These dispositifs function as authoritative mechanisms for broadening and deepening the understanding and engagement with cultural content. Examples include institutional publications, official social media campaigns, lectures, academic conferences, and collaborative projects with other cultural or educational institutions. The institutional nature of these dispositifs underscores their role in delivering institutionally legitimized means for enriching and expanding the cultural narrative and intellectual engagement."@en .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Institutional_Expansion_Dispositif> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "dispositivo de expansión institucional"@es .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Institutional_Expansion_Dispositif> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "institutional expansion dispositif"@en .
# 
# https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Institutional_Mediation_Dispositif
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Institutional_Mediation_Dispositif> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Institutional_Mediation_Dispositif> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Mediation_Dispositif> .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Institutional_Mediation_Dispositif> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "The \"Institutional Mediation Dispositif\" class represents official materials and activities used by institutions to facilitate the interpretation, understanding, and engagement with cultural activities and productions. These dispositifs function as authoritative mechanisms for guiding public interaction with cultural content, providing structured and sanctioned methods of mediation. Examples include institutional educational programs, guided tours, official exhibition catalogs, interpretive panels, interactive displays, and workshops organized by the institution. The institutional nature of these dispositifs underscores their role in institutionally approved interpretations and educational content to the public."@en .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Institutional_Mediation_Dispositif> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "dispositivo de mediación institucional"@es .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Institutional_Mediation_Dispositif> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "institutional mediation dispositif"@en .
# 
# https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Instructor
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Instructor> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Instructor> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Role> .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Instructor> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "instructor"@en .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Instructor> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "instructor/a"@es .
# 
# https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Interpretation
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Interpretation> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Interpretation> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Proposition> .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Interpretation> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "An interpretive proposition: a statement that assigns meaning or a reading to an entity, set, or situation, potentially coexisting with alternative interpretations."@en .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Interpretation> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "interpretación"@es .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Interpretation> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "interpretation"@en .
# 
# https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Interpretation_Assignment
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Interpretation_Assignment> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Interpretation_Assignment> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Proposition_Assignment> .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Interpretation_Assignment> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "The \"Interpretation Assignment\" class represents the act or process of assigning a specific interpretation, meaning, or analysis to an entity or work. This class encompasses various activities involved in the contextualization, explanation, and understanding of an entity's significance, including the assignment of thematic, historical, cultural, or conceptual interpretations. Interpretation Assignments can be applied to artworks, texts, events, or any other entities that require deeper analysis and understanding."@en .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Interpretation_Assignment> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "asignación de interpretación"@es .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Interpretation_Assignment> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "interpretation assignment"@en .
# 
# https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Interpretation_Center
