_:genid228 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#List> .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Location> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "Location is an intermediary (reified) spatio-temporal context that links a collective actant (e.g., a group, community, or corporate body) to the place(s) where it is situated during a specific time range. It is designed to represent organisational location as historically variable rather than as a fixed attribute, allowing multiple location records to coexist for the same entity across different periods.\n\nIn OntoExhibit, Location is primarily used to connect collective actants to geographical territories or techno-digital places that function as their institutional points of presence (for example, a headquarters city and an official website or platform). This makes it possible to describe complementary forms of location—physical and techno-digital—without conflating them with broader activity geographies. In contrast to Place of Activity, which captures the distributed set of places where an actant operates professionally, Location captures where an organisation is formally situated in a given period; for individual actants, the analogous biographical construct is Residency."@en .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Location> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "emplazamiento"@es .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Location> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "location"@en .
