# 
# https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Narrative_Thematic_Unit
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Narrative_Thematic_Unit> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Narrative_Thematic_Unit> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/7.1.1/E28_Conceptual_Object> .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Narrative_Thematic_Unit> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "The discursive and/or narrative parts through which an intellectual work is articulated. This is an explicit segmentation that may develop one or more propositions."@en .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Narrative_Thematic_Unit> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "narrative/discursive unit"@en .
# 
# https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Natural_Landscape
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Natural_Landscape> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Natural_Landscape> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#physical_place> .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Natural_Landscape> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "espacio natural"@es .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Natural_Landscape> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "natural landscape"@en .
# 
# https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Natural_Object
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Natural_Object> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Natural_Object> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Non_Produced_Material_Object> .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Natural_Object> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "The \"Natural Object\" class represents entities that occur naturally in the environment. These objects are not created by humans or technological processes, and include a wide range of naturally occurring materials and formations such as rocks, minerals, plants, fossils, and other elements found in nature. Natural objects can be part of art exhibitions. They may be exhibited for their intrinsic beauty, scientific interest, or cultural significance. Therefore, they can also be integral components of artworks or art installations."@en .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Natural_Object> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "natural object"@en .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Natural_Object> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "objeto de la naturaleza"@es .
# 
# https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Non-Institutional_Communication_Dispositif
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Non-Institutional_Communication_Dispositif> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Non-Institutional_Communication_Dispositif> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Communication_Dispositif> .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Non-Institutional_Communication_Dispositif> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "The \"Non-Institutional Communication Dispositif\" class represents unofficial materials and events used to communicate and disseminate information about cultural activities and productions. These dispositifs often include contributions from individuals, artists, independent groups, and grassroots organizations. They may reflect personal perspectives or community-driven initiatives. Examples include personal blogs, independent social media posts, artist statements, community newsletters, zines, and other forms of informal communication. The non-institutional nature of these dispositifs underscores their role in offering diverse and decentralized forms of communication within the cultural sector."@en .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Non-Institutional_Communication_Dispositif> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "dispositivo de comunicación no institucional"@es .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Non-Institutional_Communication_Dispositif> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "non-institutional communication dispositif"@en .
# 
# https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Non-Institutional_Documentation_Dispositif
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Non-Institutional_Documentation_Dispositif> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Non-Institutional_Documentation_Dispositif> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Documentation_Dispositif> .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Non-Institutional_Documentation_Dispositif> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "The \"Non-Institutional Documentation Dispositif\" class represents unofficial documentation tools and mechanisms used to record and interpret information about events, activities, and objects. These dispositifs often include socially constructed materials, contributions from artists, and other informal or non-official sources. They may reflect personal perspectives, creative interpretations, or community-generated content, providing alternative narratives and insights that complement or challenge institutional records. Examples include personal archives, artist's notebooks, community-led documentation projects, and other forms of grassroots or independent documentation."@en .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Non-Institutional_Documentation_Dispositif> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "dispositivo de documentación no institucional"@es .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Non-Institutional_Documentation_Dispositif> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "non-institutional documentation dispositif"@en .
# 
# https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Non-Institutional_Expansion_Dispositif
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Non-Institutional_Expansion_Dispositif> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Non-Institutional_Expansion_Dispositif> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Expansion_Dispositif> .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Non-Institutional_Expansion_Dispositif> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "The \"Non-Institutional Expansion Dispositif\" class represents informal tools, materials, and activities used to expand the interpretations, readings, discourses, ideas, and understanding of cultural events or objects. These dispositifs are often created and distributed by individuals, artists, independent groups, or grassroots organizations. They provide alternative and diverse methods for broadening and deepening engagement with cultural content, reflecting personal perspectives, creative interpretations, or community-driven initiatives. Examples include independent publications, artist-led discussions, community workshops, blogs, zines, and other informal media. The non-institutional nature of these dispositifs underscores their role in offering varied and decentralized forms of expanding cultural narratives and intellectual engagement."@en .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Non-Institutional_Expansion_Dispositif> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "dispositivo de expansión no institucional"@es .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Non-Institutional_Expansion_Dispositif> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "non-institutional expansion dispositif"@en .
# 
# https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Non-Institutional_Mediation_Dispositif
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Non-Institutional_Mediation_Dispositif> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Non-Institutional_Mediation_Dispositif> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Mediation_Dispositif> .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Non-Institutional_Mediation_Dispositif> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "The \"Non-Institutional Mediation Dispositif\" class represents informal tools, materials, and activities used to facilitate the interpretation, understanding, and engagement with cultural activities and productions. These dispositifs are often created and distributed by individuals, artists, independent groups, or grassroots organizations. They provide alternative and diverse methods of mediation that reflect personal perspectives, creative interpretations, or community-driven initiatives. Examples include artist-led workshops, independent guided tours, personal blogs, community discussion groups, zines, and other forms of informal mediation. The non-institutional nature of these dispositifs underscores their role in offering varied and decentralized forms of mediation that complement or challenge official narratives."@en .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Non-Institutional_Mediation_Dispositif> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "dispositivo de mediación no institucional"@es .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Non-Institutional_Mediation_Dispositif> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "non-institutional mediation dispositif"@en .
# 
# https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Non_Human_Actant
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Non_Human_Actant> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Non_Human_Actant> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Actant> .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Non_Human_Actant> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "A non-human entity that plays an active role in the realm of art exhibitions. This may include biological entities, such as animals and plants, as well as inanimate objects, that actively participate in the creation, interpretation, or presentation of artworks and cultural productions. This class is essential for addressing the participation of non-human entities that affect or contribute to artistic dynamics in significant ways. For example, an installation may include living plants, where their biological processes (such as growth, photosynthesis, and response to light stimuli) are monitored and used to alter other elements of the installation, such as lighting, sounds, or visual projections. Sensors placed on the plants detect changes in their environment (such as humidity, temperature, and light) and these data trigger modifications in the installation, creating an environment that is co-created by the biological activity of the plants. This class does not comprise technological entities which, in the context of the OntoExhibit ontology, are considered to inherently carry a human component in their design, conceptualization, and execution. See “Techno-Actant” class."@en .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Non_Human_Actant> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "actante no humano"@es .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Non_Human_Actant> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "non-human actant"@en .
# 
# https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Non_Produced_Material_Object
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Non_Produced_Material_Object> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Non_Produced_Material_Object> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Material_Object> .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Non_Produced_Material_Object> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "The Non-Produced Material Object class represents entities that have a material existence but have not been intentionally created or manufactured. These objects occur naturally or exist independently of production. Non-Produced Material Objects include natural artifacts and biological organisms without deliberate intervention or creation."@en .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Non_Produced_Material_Object> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "non-produced material object"@en .
# 
# https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Non_Propositional_Object
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Non_Propositional_Object> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Non_Propositional_Object> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Produced_Material_Object> .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Non_Propositional_Object> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "The \"Non-Propositional Object\" class represents material entities that do not primarily serve as realizations of ideas, concepts, or conceptual works. Unlike Propositional Objects, Non-Propositional Objects are not characterized by their semantic nature. Instead, they exist primarily for their physical or digital properties, or utilitarian functions. These objects can include items and devices whose primary significance is not tied to conveying meaning or expressing concepts or ideas."@en .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Non_Propositional_Object> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "non-propositional object"@en .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Non_Propositional_Object> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "objeto no proposicional"@es .
# 
# https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Object
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Object> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Object> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "object"@en .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Object> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "objeto"@es .
# 
# https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Open_Day
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Open_Day> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Open_Day> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Communication_Activity> .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Open_Day> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> _:genid238 .
_:genid238 <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#allValuesFrom> <https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Date> .
_:genid238 <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#onProperty> <https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#hasTimeSpan> .
_:genid238 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Restriction> .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Open_Day> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "The \"Open Day\" class represents an event where an institution, such as a museum, gallery, or cultural organization, opens its doors to the public, often free of charge, to provide access to exhibitions, facilities, and special activities. Open Days are designed to engage the community, attract new visitors, and provide educational and recreational opportunities. These events typically include guided tours, workshops, performances, and interactive sessions to enhance the visitor experience."@en .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Open_Day> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "jornada de puertas abiertas"@es .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Open_Day> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "open day"@en .
