# 
# https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Site
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Site> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Site> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/7.1.1/Place> .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Site> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "Represents any defined area that facilitates specific art-related activities, encompassing both physical and non-physical (digital or virtual) environments, focusing on the use and significance of each place as a venue for events, interactions, or displays. This class includes buildings, natural landscapes, outdoor areas, as well as specific digital sites that are part of larger digital platforms or virtual environments."@en .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Site> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "site"@en .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Site> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "site"@es .
# 
# https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Social_Digital_Place
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Social_Digital_Place> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Social_Digital_Place> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Digital_Place> .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Social_Digital_Place> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "The \"Social Digital Place\" class represents digital environments designed to enable social interactions, communication and social engagement among users. Social Digital Places include social media platforms, online forums, and other digital venues that enable users to connect, share, and interact in a social context. This class is of significant cultural relevance, not only due to its frequent use by cultural actors, but also due to the growing tendency to use these spaces as venues for digital exhibitions. Example includes Museo del Prado’s TikTok or exhibitions curated and displayed in Instagram."@en .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Social_Digital_Place> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "espacio digital social"@es .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Social_Digital_Place> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "social digital place"@en .
# 
# https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Social_Interaction
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Social_Interaction> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Social_Interaction> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/7.1.1/E5_Event> .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Social_Interaction> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "The \"Social Reaction\" class represents the interactions and responses that occur on social media platforms in relation to artworks, exhibitions, events, or cultural entities. This includes likes, comments, shares, mentions, and other forms of engagement that reflect the public's reaction and interaction with the content. Social Reactions provide valuable insights into audience perceptions, trends, and the overall impact of the cultural content within the social media landscape."@en .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Social_Interaction> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "interacción social"@es .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Social_Interaction> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "social interaction"@en .
# 
# https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Social_Publication
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Social_Publication> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Social_Publication> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Propositional_Object> .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Social_Publication> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "The \"Social Publication\" class represents forms of digital communication whose content it designed to be shared on social media plataforms, and to be public or semi-public consumed. This includes posts, TikTok vídeos, Instagram stories, etc. Social Publications are characterized by their interactive nature, often encouraging engagement, feedback, and sharing among users. They play a significant role in contemporary communication, social interaction, and digital culture."@en .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Social_Publication> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "publicación social"@es .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Social_Publication> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "social publication"@en .
# 
# https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Spatial_Unit
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Spatial_Unit> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Spatial_Unit> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#physical_place> .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Spatial_Unit> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "Represents a specific segment or part of a larger spatial structure that serves a distinct function or holds significance within the overall environment. This class includes rooms, halls, wings, or any other designated areas within buildings, complexes, or other types of larger spaces. For example: the “Sala Noble” (spatial unit) is the spatial unit of Palacio de Villalón (Building – physical site), which is the headquarters of Museo Carmen Thyssen (Museum – cultural institution)."@en .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Spatial_Unit> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "spatial unit"@en .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Spatial_Unit> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "unidad espacial"@es .
# 
# https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Supporter
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Supporter> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Supporter> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Role> .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Supporter> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "patrocinador"@es .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Supporter> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "sponsor"@en .
# 
# https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Techno-Actant
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Techno-Actant> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Techno-Actant> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Actant> .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Techno-Actant> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Computational_Object> .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Techno-Actant> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> _:genid246 .
_:genid246 <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#allValuesFrom> _:genid247 .
_:genid247 <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#unionOf> _:genid250 .
_:genid250 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#rest> _:genid249 .
_:genid249 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#rest> _:genid248 .
_:genid248 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#rest> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#nil> .
_:genid248 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#first> <https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Creator> .
_:genid248 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#List> .
_:genid249 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#first> <https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Collaborator> .
_:genid249 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#List> .
_:genid250 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#first> <https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Assistant> .
_:genid250 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#List> .
_:genid247 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
_:genid246 <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#onProperty> <https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#hasRole> .
_:genid246 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Restriction> .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Techno-Actant> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "A technological entity or digital and/or computational object that actively participates in the artistic process or in the conceptualization, design, development, presentation, or interpretation of exhibitions. It may include software, hardware, and automated systems. The incorporation of this class allows OntoExhibit to capture and analyze the integration and impact of advanced technologies, especially AI systems in their multiple modalities, providing a framework to understand how these technological entities modify and affect the realm of artistic exhibitions and curatorial projects. Unlike the Non-Human Actant class, in the context of the OntoExhibit ontology, it is considered that technological entities and computational and/or digital objects inherently enclosed a human component in their design, conceptualization, development, and execution, and therefore cannot be considered non-human entities in a strict sense. This class is included without prejudice to the fact that further discussion about the role these entities play in the field of artistic and curatorial production and creation, and their agency value (their possibilities and their limits) must be developed in the coming years."@en .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Techno-Actant> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "techno-actant"@en .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Techno-Actant> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "tecnoactante"@es .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Techno-Actant> <https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#modelingNote> "Techno-Actant is treated as a computational object and therefore as a propositional object: its modelling, rules, and operational logics embed explicit or implicit propositions that can intervene in exhibition ecologies (e.g., by producing, filtering, ordering, recommending, or moderating). Its affiliation to Actant expresses an attributed-agency facet in context—how it is referenced as participating in action—without implying personhood and without overriding its status as a produced computational object. This enables research questions such as:  “What implicit propositions about authorship, responsibility, and curatorial agency are embedded in the techno-actant used by exhibition X?” or “How do two techno-actants differ in the propositions they enact through their operational logics—e.g., what they generate, filter, rank, recommend, or moderate—and with what effects on visibility and interpretation?”."@en .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Techno-Actant> <https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#roce_facet> <https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Actant> .
# 
# https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Techno-Place
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Techno-Place> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Techno-Place> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/7.1.1/Place> .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Techno-Place> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Computational_Object> .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Techno-Place> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "Represents any virtual or digital environment that is created, enhanced, or enabled through technological means. In OntoExhibit, the techno-place class is a comprehensive class that comprises the variety of digital and/or technological spaces where the digital and/or computational activities of agents within the art exhibition domain are carried out."@en .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Techno-Place> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "techno-place"@en .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Techno-Place> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "tecnolugar"@es .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Techno-Place> <https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#modelingNote> "Techno-Place is treated as a computational object and therefore as a propositional object: its design, data models, rules, affordances, and governance embed explicit or implicit propositions that shape what can appear, circulate, and be accessed. Its affiliation to Place expresses a facet of use and reference—how it functions as a locus for exhibition-related interactions and processes—without denying its objecthood or its propositional character. This enables research questions such as:  “What implicit propositions about audiences/participation are embedded in the platform used by exhibition X?” or “How do the propositions of two techno-places differ in their navigation design, permissions, and visibility regimes?”"@en .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Techno-Place> <https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#roce_facet> <https://cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/7.1.1/Place> .
# 
# https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Technological_Corporation
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Technological_Corporation> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Technological_Corporation> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Company> .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Technological_Corporation> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "corporación tecnológica"@es .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Technological_Corporation> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "technological corporation"@en .
# 
# https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Territorial_Entity
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Territorial_Entity> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Territorial_Entity> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/7.1.1/Place> .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Territorial_Entity> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "This class represents any defined area distinguished by administrative or geopolitical boundaries. This class encompasses a wide range of entities from cities and municipalities to states and countries. Each instance of a Territorial Entity is characterized by its governance structures and the legal and political recognition it holds. This class is crucial for analyzing and documenting the geopolitical movement of agents and objects, and for analyzing the influence and interactions of various levels of governance in cultural contexts."@en .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Territorial_Entity> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "entidad territorial"@es .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Territorial_Entity> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "territorial entity"@en .
# 
# https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Theme
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Theme> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Theme> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/7.1.1/E28_Conceptual_Object> .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Theme> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "A conceptual framework or abstract category used to organize, group, or contextualize content, such as artworks, exhibitions, or narratives. Themes provide a unifying idea or focus, serving as a basis for interpretation, but they are not inherently propositional or subject to verification. E.g.: \"The Exploration of Identity Through Modern Art Movements\"."@en .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Theme> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "tema"@es .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Theme> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "theme"@en .
# 
# https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Thesis
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Thesis> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Thesis> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Proposition> .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Thesis> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "A proposition formulated as a thesis: a defendable statement that articulates a central claim or argument within critical, curatorial, scholarly, or institutional discourse."@en .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Thesis> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "tesis"@es .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Thesis> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "thesis"@en .
# 
# https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Time_Range
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Time_Range> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Time_Range> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Time_Span> .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Time_Range> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "This class represents a span of time characterized by a specific start date and end date, capturing the duration between two defined points tin time."@en .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Time_Range> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "rango temporal"@es .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Time_Range> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "time range"@en .
# 
# https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Time_Span
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Time_Span> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Time_Span> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "The \"Time Span\" class represents a continuous duration or interval of time. This class is used to capture the temporal extent of events, activities, processes, or any phenomena that occur over a period of time. A Time Span can range from very short durations, such as seconds or minutes, to extended periods, such as years, decades, or even millennia."@en .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Time_Span> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "periodo de tiempo"@es .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Time_Span> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "time-span"@en .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Time_Span> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#equivalentClass> "https://cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/7.1.1/E52_Time-Span"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#anyURI> .
# 
# https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Title
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Title> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Title> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Linguistic_Information_Object> .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Title> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "title"@en .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Title> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "título"@es .
# 
# https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Tour
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Tour> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Tour> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Scholarly_Educational_Activity> .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Tour> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "tour"@en .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Tour> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "tour"@es .
# 
# https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Triennial
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Triennial> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Triennial> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Exhibition> .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Triennial> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "The \"Triennial\" class represents a large-scale exhibition event that occurs every three years, showcasing a curated selection of artworks, often from a diverse range of artists, disciplines, and cultural backgrounds. Triennials are significant events in the art world, designed to present contemporary art, foster international cultural exchange, and engage with global artistic trends. These events typically span several months and include various programs such as exhibitions, performances, panel discussions, workshops, and educational activities."@en .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Triennial> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "trienal"@es .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Triennial> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "triennial"@en .
# 
# https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#University
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#University> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#University> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Educational_Institution> .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#University> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "universidad"@es .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#University> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "university"@en .
