# https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#biographical_context
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#biographical_context> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#biographical_context> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#spatio-temporal_context> .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#biographical_context> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "Biographical context is a subclass of Spatio-temporal context used to model time-qualified circumstances that shape an individual’s life history and trajectory. It groups intermediary constructs that connect a person to places, situations, or other biographical conditions while accounting for change over time (e.g., moving between residences or shifting life settings). Rather than treating such relations as fixed attributes of a person, Biographical context represents them as contextual records anchored to a defined time range, enabling the reconstruction and analysis of biographical geographies and temporal sequences."@en .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#biographical_context> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "biographical context"@en .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#biographical_context> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "contexto biográfico"@es .
# 
# https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#headquarter_assignment
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#headquarter_assignment> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#headquarter_assignment> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#institutional_center_assignment> .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#headquarter_assignment> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "Headquarter is an intermediary (reified) spatio-temporal context that links a collective actant (e.g., a group, community, or corporate body) to its principal physical operational site for a given time range. It represents headquarters information as historically contingent, allowing organisations to be associated with different main addresses or sites across successive periods rather than treating headquarters as a permanent attribute.\n\nIn OntoExhibit, Headquarter is intended for cases—especially common in cultural organisations such as galleries—where the official physical base of operations changes over time. It should be distinguished from Location, which may record multiple simultaneous forms of organisational presence (including techno-digital places), and from Activity Place, which captures the wider set of places where an actant conducts activities beyond its principal site.."@en .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#headquarter_assignment> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "headquarter"@en .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#headquarter_assignment> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "sede central"@es .
# 
# https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#institutional_center_assignment
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#institutional_center_assignment> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#institutional_center_assignment> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#institutional_context> .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#institutional_center_assignment> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "Is an intermediary (reified) spatio-temporal context that links a collective actant to a physical place functioning as an institutional facility within a defined time range. It captures the organisational role of a physical site (as part of an institution’s infrastructure) rather than treating the site itself as a type of institution. Instances of Institutional center support the modelling of institutions with multiple facilities and changing spatial infrastructures over time, enabling analysis of institutional footprints, expansions, relocations, and multi-site configurations."@en .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#institutional_center_assignment> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "centro institucional"@es .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#institutional_center_assignment> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "institutional center"@en .
# 
# https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#institutional_context
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#institutional_context> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#institutional_context> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#spatio-temporal_context> .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#institutional_context> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "is a subclass of Spatio-temporal context that groups intermediary (reified) constructs used to describe time-qualified institutional configurations and organisational circumstances. It is intended to model historically variable institutional arrangements—such as official locations, centres, and infrastructural presences—without reducing them to timeless attributes of the organisation or to the intrinsic nature of places. In OntoExhibit, Institutional context provides a shared pattern for representing how collective actants are situated and structured across time through formal, institutionally recognised relations to physical and/or techno-digital places."@es .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#institutional_context> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "contexto institucional"@es .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#institutional_context> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "institutional context"@en .
# 
# https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#newspaper
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#newspaper> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#newspaper> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Information_Object> .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#newspaper> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "newspaper"@en .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#newspaper> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "periódico"@es .
# 
# https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#ordering_principle
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#ordering_principle> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#ordering_principle> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/7.1.1/E28_Conceptual_Object> .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#ordering_principle> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "Ordering Principle designates an abstract principle that governs how elements are ordered, sequenced, grouped, or prioritized in an intellectual work or exhibition-related articulation (e.g., chronological, thematic, geographic, typological, algorithmic). It expresses the logic of ordering, independently of the particular structure that realizes it."@en .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#ordering_principle> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "ordering principle"@en .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#ordering_principle> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "principio de ordenación"@es .
# 
# https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#physical_place
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#physical_place> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#physical_place> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/7.1.1/Place> .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#physical_place> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "espacio físico"@es .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#physical_place> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "physical place"@en .
# 
# https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#process_locus
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#process_locus> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#process_locus> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/7.1.1/Place> .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#process_locus> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "Process Locus designates a place understood in its role as the spatial (territorial or techno-digital) locus of a process relevant to curation and/or exhibition making and its associated chains of cultural production. Rather than defining a place by its intrinsic nature (e.g., territory, building, platform), this class captures a contextual function: a place becomes a Process Locus when it is referenced as the location where specific processes occur—such as conception, curation, creation, foundation, dissolution, or destruction—within the broader ecology of exhibitions, artworks, institutions, and cultural agents.\n\nIn OntoExhibit, Process Locus is introduced to make explicit the often distributed and multi-sited character of exhibition work. It enables the ontology to distinguish between the frontstage geography of display (e.g., venues/sites where exhibitions are presented) and the backstage geography of production and organization (e.g., where curatorial, creative, and institutional processes are carried out, which may be remote, outsourced, or platform-based). A single place (for instance, a city, a workshop, a museum building, or an online environment) may function as a Process Locus in some contexts and not in others; therefore, Process Locus should not be interpreted as a permanent subtype of place, but as a role inferred from its participation in process-location relations.\n\nBy providing a shared conceptual handle over heterogeneous place types, Process Locus supports comparative analysis across cases (e.g., identifying off-site or techno-digital loci of conception and curation, mapping distributed production networks, and measuring degrees of spatial decoupling between production and exhibition). This makes it particularly useful for exhibition studies and curatorial research focused on infrastructures, labor, and the spatial politics of cultural production."@en .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#process_locus> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "lugar de procesos"@es .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#process_locus> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "process locus"@en .
# 
# https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#professional_context
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#professional_context> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#professional_context> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#spatio-temporal_context> .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#professional_context> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "Professional context is a subclass of Spatio-temporal context used to model time-qualified relations through which actants participate in professional, institutional, and organisational environments. It groups intermediary constructs that connect people or collectives to places of activity, memberships, affiliations, representative roles, or executive positions, capturing how these relations evolve across time and (when relevant) across space. By reifying these links as contextual records, Professional context supports analyses of careers, networks, institutional trajectories, and the shifting infrastructures through which cultural work is organised within exhibition ecosystems."@en .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#professional_context> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "contexto profesional"@es .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#professional_context> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "professional context"@en .
# 
# https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#propositional_activity
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#propositional_activity> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#propositional_activity> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Planned_Activity> .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#propositional_activity> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#Propositional_Entity> .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#propositional_activity> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "Propositional Activity designates a planned activity whose defining component includes one or more propositions articulated as its propositional content. It covers activities in which statements, arguments, interpretations, or discursive framings are central to what the activity is and does, enabling them to be related to \"Proposition\" via \"conveys\" within OntoExhibit."@en .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#propositional_activity> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "actividad proposicional"@es .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#propositional_activity> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "propositional activity"@en .
# 
# https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#secondary_center_assignment
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#secondary_center_assignment> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#secondary_center_assignment> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#institutional_center_assignment> .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#secondary_center_assignment> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "This class refers to a subsidiary or additional facility of an institution, distinct from its main headquarters. This center may be the place of activities related to the institution's mission, providing supplementary services, exhibitions, or resources. It usually serves as an extension of the institution, enhancing its reach and capabilities. Example: \"The Villanueva building serve as the headquarters of the Museo del Prado, whereas the Casón del Buen Retiro serves as Secondary Center.\""@en .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#secondary_center_assignment> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "secondary center"@en .
<https://w3id.org/OntoExhibit#secondary_center_assignment> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "sede secundaria"@es .
